analysis:
The only change is in docs/architecture/prefix-cache.md at line 689 (base and head). It edits one row of the native-checkpoint size table for gpt-oss-20b. The row changes from float32 K/V on 23 of 24 layers (6.03 MB fixed, 50.33 MB per 1,024 tokens, 308.0 MB at 6,144 tokens) to bf16 K/V on all 24 layers (3.15 MB, 25.17 MB, 154.1 MB).

The change is documentation only. It does not alter any control relevant to T-041 or SEC-035, including:
- encryption, HMAC naming, cacheSalt scoping or TTL;
- disk budget or the per-donation gate;
- the staging caps (CBv2HistoricalStagingCap, 1/8 and 1/16 limits);
- identity bindings or kill switches.

The new figures are internally consistent. 24,576 B/token × 1,024 ≈ 25.17 MB, and 6 × 25.17 + 3.15 ≈ 154.1 MB. Smaller per-file sizes would, if anything, reduce the documented staging and at-rest footprint. They are illustrative and are not budget enforcement inputs.

One point is unverified. The supplied evidence does not show whether the runtime dtype probe actually observes bf16 K/V for gpt-oss-20b. Line 100 notes that DARKBLOOM_CBV2_PAGED_KV_DTYPE accepts only float16 or float32, which may differ from bf16. This is a documentation-accuracy question, not a security regression. The surrounding unchanged text was reviewed for context only; pre-existing issues were not restated.
